  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure south of the area will push a warm\nfront north through the region Tuesday night through Wednesday.\n\nA cold front currently working its way through the region will \nwash out over the area on Tuesday. High pressure builds in from \nthe north and west on Tuesday before departing Tuesday night. A \npleasant day on tap for Tuesday with highs in the low to mid 80s\nand dew points generally in the low 60s.\n\nA warm front then lifts north through the region Tuesday night,\nallowing showers and isolated thunderstorms to develop late \nTuesday night and into Wednesday morning. Low pressure \ndeveloping over the Southeast lifts north along the Eastern \nSeaboard, but there is considerable uncertainty as to its track.\n12Z GFS takes it out to sea with minimal impacts to the area, \nwhile the 12Z ECMWF as it inland across Pennsylvania, where the \nheaviest rain will fall, and the 12Z NAM brings it right over \nthe I-95 corridor. Heavy rain will result in flash flooding, but\nit is uncertain as to where that heavy rain will fall. The \nWeather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall, which is a 1 out of 4. For now, will follow the NBM \nand carry high chance to low end likely PoPs for most of the \nregion. There is the potential for another 1/2 inch to 1 inch of\nrain Wednesday afternoon and evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...For the later half of the week, we will have a \nhot, humid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nExpect a prolonged period of southwesterly flow as our region \nwill be in between a Bermuda High well to our east, and a mid \nlevel long wave trough that will be slowly sliding east over the\nnorthern tier of the U.S./southern Canada. As such, heat and \nhumidity will be building throughout the later half of the week.\nAt this point, Friday and Saturday appear to be the warmest \ndays, with highs ranging from the upper 70s to near 90. That in \nand of itself is pretty close to normal. However, with dewpoints\nin the mid 70s possible, we could get heat index values at or \nabove 100 degrees for much of the area. All that being said, \nthis still doesn't look nearly as impactful an extreme heat \nsetup as what we saw earlier this summer during the first week \nof July. \n\nThe other aspect of this are the daily storm chances, primarily\nconfined to the afternoon and evening with a typical diurnal \npattern. Given the hot and humid conditions that will be in \nplace, we can't rule out the more common thunderstorm hazards of\ndamaging winds and heavy rain/flooding, but in general, I don't\nsee any one day that has an especially high threat of any of \nthese at this time. If an embedded short wave trough crosses our\nregion as the larger trough axis approaches, that could \nincrease the risk, particularly for severe threats, but trying \nto time that out at this point would be very difficult, given \nthat we are still seeing some differences between the models \nwith the larger pattern.",
